  "h1": "SSDI vs SSI Differences in Work Credits and Limits",
  "introText": "Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI) pays monthly benefits to disabled workers who earned enough work credits, while Supplemental Security Income (SSI) pays monthly benefits to aged, blind, or disabled adults with limited income and financial resources.\n\nYour work history and personal finances determine which program you can receive. Workers with recent earnings qualify for SSDI through their payroll tax records, whereas applicants without recent work history must meet strict income and asset thresholds to receive SSI under federal rules.",
