  "verdictHeading": "Our Takeaway for SSDI vs SSI",
  "verdict": "Apply for SSDI if you have a consistent work history and sufficient quarters of coverage under federal insurance rules, or apply for SSI if your assets fall below regulatory limits and you require immediate safety-net assistance. Federal regulations in 20 CFR 404.315 and 20 CFR 416.202 establish that SSDI rewards prior labor contributions while SSI provides basic financial security based purely on demonstrated economic need. If your countable resources exceed $2,000 for an individual or $3,000 for a married couple, you are disqualified from SSI regardless of medical severity, leaving SSDI as the sole viable federal disability avenue. To determine your eligibility and submit an application, review your Social Security earnings record through the [Social Security Administration](https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/) to verify your insured status for ssdi vs ssi.",
  "disclaimerText": "This comparison provides general educational information regarding federal benefit programs and does not constitute formal legal, financial, or benefits advice; all individual eligibility determinations are made solely by the Social Security Administration.",
